# PostgreSQL storage-full and restart qualification

This standalone Linux Docker drill fills only a new, explicitly bounded tmpfs
volume. It never mounts an existing database, publishes a port, pulls an image,
fills the host root filesystem or connects to customer infrastructure. Run it
on a qualification host with at least 2 GiB of unused RAM. It requires the
Docker local volume driver to support tmpfs mounts; rootless configurations
that cannot mount them must fail, not fall back to a host directory.

    python3 scripts/check_postgres_pressure.py \
      --postgres REVIEWED_LOCAL_POSTGRES_IMAGE \
      --work-dir /absolute/new/private/postgres-pressure

The image is resolved once to its local immutable ID. The default volume limit
is 512 MiB; --volume-mib accepts only 512..1024. A separate keeper container
maintains the tmpfs mount while PostgreSQL restarts. Both containers have no
network peers and explicit memory/CPU limits. The driver verifies volume
ownership, mount type, actual filesystem capacity and absence of other mounts
before deliberately consuming the remaining space.

The test seeds and checkpoints 1,000 synthetic rows, fills the verified tmpfs
to actual ENOSPC and requires a larger database transaction to fail with an
explicit PostgreSQL storage-full diagnostic. After removing only its filler
file, it restarts the database. The cluster identifier and original row
fingerprint must be unchanged, and the failed transaction must have left no
committed rows. A subsequent insert and checkpoint must succeed. An OOM
invalidates the result; memory exhaustion is not accepted as storage failure.

Only report.json is intended for sharing. Every other file in the private
0700 work directory can contain fixture credentials or raw diagnostics. Keep
failed runs and use a new work directory for each attempt. The runner removes
only resources with its exact ownership label and reports uncertain cleanup;
it never prunes Docker or deletes another deployment's resources.

This is a synthetic PostgreSQL filesystem-full/restart check. The tmpfs is
volatile; the keeper is a test mechanism, not a production persistence design.
A pass does not establish host-crash durability, physical-disk behavior,
application-level error handling, real workload capacity, HA/queue recovery,
RTO/RPO, or a safe procedure for filling or repairing a live database. The
separate CAPACITY-ACCEPTANCE drill covers report-path failure and bounded
application load; it does not turn this database check into fleet acceptance.
